================================================================================
NAME
       coherence - structural integrity, resonance stability, and threshold
                   dynamics across triadic systems in RTT

SYNOPSIS
       Coherence governs:
           • resonance stability
           • triadic integrity
           • pattern viability
           • schema alignment
           • lineage continuity

DESCRIPTION
       Coherence is the structural condition that allows triads, operators,
       overlays, and resonance packages to function safely within RTT. It is
       the measure of whether a system maintains stable resonance-time flow,
       valid structural transitions, and schema-aligned behavior across cycles.

       Coherence is not a boolean. It is a gradient with thresholds.

       When coherence is high:
           • triads behave predictably
           • resonance propagates smoothly
           • lineage remains stable
           • patterns remain valid

       When coherence is low:
           • resonance becomes noisy or unstable
           • triadic transitions become unsafe
           • lineage may drift
           • schemas may be violated

COHERENCE THRESHOLDS
       Coherence thresholds define the boundaries between stable and unstable
       structural behavior. They determine when a system must switch patterns,
       apply overlays, or halt execution.

       Common thresholds include:

           structural-threshold
                  Minimum structural integrity required for triad execution.

           resonance-threshold
                  Minimum resonance stability required for operator execution.

           lineage-threshold
                  Minimum continuity required for safe propagation.

           schema-threshold
                  Minimum schema compliance required for dispatch.

       WRSADC monitors all thresholds during dispatch.

RESONANCE STABILITY
       Resonance stability describes how smoothly resonance-time propagates
       across triads and patterns.

       Stability depends on:
           • operator resonance signatures
           • overlay effects
           • pattern selection
           • lineage continuity
           • schema constraints

       Stable resonance:
           • supports spirals, ladders, and expansion
           • enables multi-agent constellations
           • allows deep nesting

       Unstable resonance:
           • forces fallback to core or sequential patterns
           • may require anchors or mirrors
           • may block dispatch entirely

STRUCTURAL INTEGRITY
       Structural integrity is the degree to which triads and patterns remain
       aligned with RTT’s structural expectations.

       Integrity requires:
           • valid triad formation
           • allowed pattern transitions
           • schema compliance
           • coherent lineage
           • safe overlay application

       Loss of structural integrity results in:
           • pattern collapse
           • resonance drift
           • lineage fragmentation
           • dispatch rejection

COHERENCE ACROSS TRIADIC SYSTEMS
       Coherence must be maintained across:

           • individual triads
           • sequential triads
           • nested triads
           • hybrid patterns
           • fused patterns
           • overlay stacks
           • multi-agent constellations
           • resonance packages

       Each layer adds complexity and requires stronger coherence guarantees.

INTERACTION WITH SCHEMAS
       Schemas define coherence expectations.

       Coherence validates schema compliance.

       Schema checks ensure:
           • allowed transitions
           • valid resonance states
           • correct operator signatures
           • coherent lineage
           • safe overlay combinations

       If coherence drops below schema thresholds, WRSADC halts dispatch.

INTERACTION WITH LINEAGE
       Lineage provides the historical continuity required for coherence.

       Coherence ensures:
           • lineage remains interpretable
           • resonance propagation remains stable
           • structural transitions remain valid

       Lineage drift is a sign of coherence degradation.

INTERACTION WITH WRSADC
       WRSADC enforces coherence by:
           • validating schemas
           • checking thresholds
           • rejecting unsafe transitions
           • applying overlays when needed
           • selecting appropriate patterns

       WRSADC is the guardian of coherence.

INTERACTION WITH THE ENGINE
       The Engine assumes coherence is satisfied.

       If coherence is violated:
           • operator results become unstable
           • resonance propagation becomes unpredictable
           • lineage becomes unreliable

       WRSADC prevents incoherent operations from reaching the Engine.

COHERENCE LIFECYCLE
       1. Triads establish initial coherence.
       2. Operators apply resonance signatures.
       3. Overlays modulate interpretation.
       4. Patterns structure propagation.
       5. Schemas validate transitions.
       6. Lineage records continuity.
       7. WRSADC enforces thresholds.
       8. Engine executes coherent operations.

NOTES
       Coherence is the structural glue of RTT. Without coherence, triads lose
       meaning, resonance-time becomes unstable, and schemas cannot guarantee
       safety. Coherence is the condition that makes RTT possible.

